  "notes": "Post-grunge radio rock with pop-chorus discipline, modelled on the straight-time singles the research names: 90–133 with the backbeat on 2&4. The down-tuned riff songs \"feel **half-time** at ~90\" — that is the other backbeat class and is named here rather than encoded, because one session block carries one. The harmonic fact that matters is a voicing one: \"power-chord voicings with no thirds, so major/minor is decided by the vocal\", which is why the triad share is 0.82 and the sevenths are almost absent — the chord generator is being asked for the plainest shapes it has. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"kick on 1 and the '&' of 2 (double-kick 8ths under the chorus)\" — a 3–6 density, the busiest kick in this batch — \"snare 2&4 with heavy gating\" (the loudest accent tier here), and \"crash on every chorus downbeat\" with \"a 16th tom run into a crash\" as the whole fill vocabulary, which is the crash/tom perc block and the tom-led fill schedule. The bass is \"locked to the kick, root notes, drop-tuned, heavily distorted\", so it runs `mirror_kick` at 0.97 with the register floor at MIDI 26 and the chord voicing lifted clear of it. ⚠ `confidence` is medium because the research rates it high-medium; it also notes that the producer-side lineage overlaps the vol. 1 Joey Moi entry, which is referenced there and not duplicated here. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are all drum probabilities and `bpm.mode` 105.",
